### What is the Set Matrix Zeroes problem?

Given an m-by-n matrix, if any cell is zero, set its entire row and entire column to zero. The challenge is to do it in place using O(1) extra space. The naive approach copies the matrix or keeps separate sets of row and column indices, costing O(m+n) extra space.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SetMatrixZeroes {
    public void setZeroes(int[][] matrix) {
        int m = matrix.length, n = matrix[0].length;
        boolean firstRowZero = false, firstColZero = false;

        // Phase 1: save flags
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
            if (matrix[0][j] == 0) firstRowZero = true;
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++)
            if (matrix[i][0] == 0) firstColZero = true;

        // Phase 2: mark using first row/column
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++)
                if (matrix[i][j] == 0) {
                    matrix[i][0] = 0;
                    matrix[0][j] = 0;
                }

        // Phase 3: zero inner cells
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++)
                if (matrix[i][0] == 0 || matrix[0][j] == 0)
                    matrix[i][j] = 0;

        // Phase 4: zero first row and column
        if (firstRowZero)
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) matrix[0][j] = 0;
        if (firstColZero)
            for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) matrix[i][0] = 0;
    }
}
```

### Why use the first row and column as markers?

Instead of allocating separate arrays or sets to record which rows and columns must be zeroed, we reuse the first row and first column of the matrix itself. When we find `matrix[i][j] == 0`, we set `matrix[i][0] = 0` (mark the row) and `matrix[0][j] = 0` (mark the column). This saves O(m+n) space.

```java
// Instead of:
Set<Integer> zeroRows = new HashSet<>();  // O(m) space
Set<Integer> zeroCols = new HashSet<>();  // O(n) space

// We use:
matrix[i][0] = 0;  // marks row i for zeroing
matrix[0][j] = 0;  // marks col j for zeroing
// Only O(1) extra space for the two boolean flags
```

### Why do we need the two boolean flags?

Before writing markers into the first row and column, we must check whether those rows and columns originally contained a zero. If we skip this step, we cannot tell whether a zero in the first row or column was an original zero or a marker we wrote. The flags capture the original state before we modify anything.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FlagDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] matrix = {
            {0, 1, 1},
            {1, 1, 1},
            {1, 1, 0}
        };
        // First row contains a zero at [0][0] -> firstRowZero = true
        // First col contains a zero at [0][0] -> firstColZero = true
        // Without flags, we would not know if matrix[0][0]=0 was
        // original or a marker from matrix[2][2]=0
        boolean firstRowZero = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++)
            if (matrix[0][j] == 0) { firstRowZero = true; break; }
        System.out.println("First row has zero: " + firstRowZero); // true
    }
}
```

```text
First row has zero: true
```

### Why does the order of phases matter?

If we zeroed the first row and column before processing the inner cells, we would destroy the markers and zero the entire matrix. The correct order is: save flags, mark, zero inner cells, then zero first row and column.

```java
// WRONG ORDER: zeroing first row/col before inner cells
// destroys markers -> entire matrix becomes zero

// CORRECT ORDER:
// Phase 1: Save flags (firstRowZero, firstColZero)
// Phase 2: Mark (write zeros into first row/col)
// Phase 3: Zero inner cells (read markers, zero cells)
// Phase 4: Zero first row/col (use saved flags)
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The matrix is traversed a constant number of times (about 3-4 passes), so time is O(m * n). Only two boolean variables are used for extra space.

- **Time complexity** – O(m * n), each cell visited a constant number of times.
- **Space complexity** – O(1) extra, using only two boolean flags.
- **Comparison** – the naive approach uses O(m + n) for row and column index sets.

```java
// Phase 1: O(m + n) to check first row and column
// Phase 2: O(m * n) to scan inner matrix and mark
// Phase 3: O(m * n) to zero inner cells
// Phase 4: O(m + n) to zero first row/column
// Total: O(m * n)
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a matrix with no zeros (nothing changes), a matrix that is entirely zero, a single-row or single-column matrix, and the case where the zero is only in the first row or first column.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ZeroEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SetMatrixZeroes solver = new SetMatrixZeroes();

        // No zeros: nothing changes
        int[][] m1 = {{1,2},{3,4}};
        solver.setZeroes(m1);
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.deepToString(m1));
        // [[1, 2], [3, 4]]

        // Single row with zero
        int[][] m2 = {{1, 0, 3}};
        solver.setZeroes(m2);
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.deepToString(m2));
        // [[0, 0, 0]]
    }
}
```

```text
[[1, 2], [3, 4]]
[[0, 0, 0]]
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is zeroing the first row and column before using the markers to zero inner cells. Another mistake is not saving the boolean flags before the marking phase, causing the original state of the first row and column to be lost.

```java
// WRONG: zero first row/col too early
if (firstRowZero)
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) matrix[0][j] = 0; // destroys markers!
for (int i = 1; i < m; i++)
    for (int j = 1; j < n; j++)
        if (matrix[i][0] == 0 || matrix[0][j] == 0)
            matrix[i][j] = 0; // reads destroyed markers!

// CORRECT: zero inner cells FIRST, then first row/col
for (int i = 1; i < m; i++)
    for (int j = 1; j < n; j++)
        if (matrix[i][0] == 0 || matrix[0][j] == 0)
            matrix[i][j] = 0;
if (firstRowZero)
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) matrix[0][j] = 0;
```

### How would you apply this in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, a reconciliation grid has rows for dates and columns for branches. A zero means a detected discrepancy that policy requires quarantining across the whole date and the whole branch before filing. The in-place marker technique processes large grids without allocating a second matrix.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ReconciliationQuarantine {
    public void quarantineDiscrepancies(int[][] grid) {
        // Reuse first row/col as markers
        int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
        boolean firstRowFlag = false, firstColFlag = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
            if (grid[0][j] == 0) firstRowFlag = true;
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++)
            if (grid[i][0] == 0) firstColFlag = true;
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++)
                if (grid[i][j] == 0) {
                    grid[i][0] = 0;
                    grid[0][j] = 0;
                }
        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++)
                if (grid[i][0] == 0 || grid[0][j] == 0)
                    grid[i][j] = 0;
        if (firstRowFlag)
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) grid[0][j] = 0;
        if (firstColFlag)
            for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) grid[i][0] = 0;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know the four-phase approach and the critical ordering constraint. They should also recognise that a single-flag variant exists where `matrix[0][0]` serves as the row-zero flag and only one extra boolean is needed for the column, but this is harder to reason about and error-prone. The two-flag version is clearer and safer.

```java
// Single-flag variant (trickier):
// matrix[0][0] is shared by first row and first col.
// Use matrix[0][0] for first row, separate flag for first col.
// Process first col flag BEFORE marking to avoid corruption.
// Two-flag version is simpler and less error-prone.
```
